1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"example-before-method","number":"04","title":"Give the model a concrete case first.","mode":"Generality first","scale":"talk","dek":"Start with an actual insurance contract, then explain the model that evaluates it.","point":"The source presents its contracts after the generic budget and estimation. Both sides include the same payment calculation; the revision moves the contract and calculation to the model’s entrance.","sourceType":"public","source":{"authors":"Amanda E. Kowalski","title":"Estimating the Tradeoff Between Risk Protection and Moral Hazard with a Nonlinear Budget Set Model of Health Insurance","date":"October 2012","url":"https://users.nber.org/~kowalski/nlbspres10_25_12.pdf","pages":"selected slides from 2–46","physicalPages":46},"before":[],"beforeCaption":"Selected milestones in source order; intervening slides omitted.","afterCaption":"The $1,000 bill is a worked example of the offered contract.","after":[],"diagnosis":"The talk introduces the generic insurance budget before the contracts in its data. One worked payment example gives the later symbols a concrete meaning.","changes":["Introduce one offered contract.","Work out who pays for a particular spending total.","Generalize its payment schedule into the model’s budget."],"preserved":"The offered-plan parameters and three-segment budget. The payment example is arithmetic, not an estimated treatment effect.","deferred":"Other contracts, virtual-income intercepts, full utility specification, and estimation details stay available in the source and outline.","practice":"Make one real instance of your model’s object understandable before introducing the general notation.","principle":"Choose detail that carries insight","context":"Actual research talk, October 2012. It already motivates the welfare question on pp. 2–3 and previews fit on p. 5. This repair places the concrete contract before the generic diagram. The study uses 2004 data from one retail employer, with 101,343 individuals in families of three or fewer. Income and premiums are not directly observed.","values":["Source parameters: F=1,D=$350,C=0.2,S=$2,100.","At $1,000 total spending,patient payment=$350+0.2×$650=$480; insurer payment=$520.","The cap starts at total spending $350+($2,100−$350)/0.2=$9,100.","P(Q) is a newly introduced notation; A+P(Q)=Y−m is equivalent to the source’s budget.","The illustrative bill and plotted schedule are computed from the contract,not observed or estimated outcomes."],"outline":{"title":"Actual contract before generic model","description":"A 46-page research presentation, grouped into seven source blocks. The proposed route starts the model with a contract from the data.","before":[{"label":"Source section","title":"Question and preview","content":"Insurance trades financial protection against changed incentives to spend; a generic budget and a fit preview introduce the approach.","pages":"2–6","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Question and preview","panels":[{"kind":"outline-stage","title":"Question and preview","label":"Question and preview","lines":["Insurance trades financial protection against changed incentives to spend; a generic budget and a fit preview introduce the approach."],"id":"insurance-question"},{"kind":"image","title":"Generic budget · PDF p. 4","alt":"A generic insurance budget uses symbolic deductibles, coinsurance, payment caps, and virtual-income intercepts before the offered plan parameters appear.","image":"/originals/kowalski-2012-p04.png","sourcePage":"4","id":"generic-budget"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Model derivation","content":"Plan choice, medical spending, welfare measures, functional form, and identification.","pages":"7–16","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Model derivation","panels":[{"id":"insurance-budget","kind":"text","title":"Budget constraint","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","formula":"A+P(Q)=Y-m","sourcePage":"4, 7–9","lines":["\\(Q\\): total medical spending · \\(P(Q)\\): patient payment","\\(A\\): other goods · \\(Y\\): income · \\(m\\): premium","Patient payments leave less money for other goods."]},{"id":"insurance-timing","kind":"text","title":"Plan choice and spending","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","sourcePage":"7, 9–12","lines":["Choose a plan before the health shock.","Choose medical spending after the shock.","Compare risk-protection gains with the cost of changed medical spending."]}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Estimation algorithm","content":"A simulation procedure connects plan choices and medical spending with the model’s parameters.","pages":"17–18","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Estimation","id":"insurance-estimation"},{"label":"Source section","title":"Data and contracts","content":"One employer, four offered plans, plan choice, summary statistics, and reduced-form tests.","pages":"19–26","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Data and contracts","panels":[{"kind":"outline-stage","title":"Data and contracts","label":"Data and contracts","lines":["One retail employer offered four plans. The $350-deductible plan charges 100% before the deductible and 20% afterward, with a $2,100 patient-payment cap."],"id":"insurance-data"},{"kind":"image","title":"Actual contracts · PDF p. 20","alt":"Four offered plans. The $350-deductible plan has 100% patient payment before the deductible,20% coinsurance afterward,and a $2,100 payment cap.","image":"/originals/kowalski-2012-p20.png","sourcePage":"20","id":"insurance-contracts"},{"id":"insurance-arithmetic","kind":"text","title":"The $350 contract: payments","provenance":"Worked example from the source contract · schematic","note":"Payments exclude the premium.","sourcePage":"20","lines":["Patient marginal payment: $1 per dollar up to $350; then 20¢; then $0 after the cap.","At $1,000 total spending: patient pays $350 + 20% × $650 = $480; insurer pays $520.","The $2,100 cap starts at $350 + ($2,100 − $350) / 0.2 = $9,100 of total spending."]}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Estimates and fit","content":"Estimated parameters imply spending responses. Actual and predicted spending are compared in regressions, budget-segment shares, and distribution plots; the fit is imperfect.","pages":"27–31","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Estimates and fit","id":"insurance-fit"},{"label":"Source section","title":"Contract counterfactuals","content":"The same population faces existing or hypothetical plans in the estimated model.","pages":"32–38","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Counterfactuals","id":"insurance-counterfactuals"},{"label":"Source section","title":"Findings and scope","content":"In existing plans, average model-implied moral-hazard costs exceed risk-protection gains, with substantial variation across individuals. The sample is already insured and does not include large expenditure shocks.","pages":"39–40; post-conclusion 41–46","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Findings and scope","id":"insurance-finding"}],"after":[{"label":"","title":"Insurance changes both risk and incentives.","content":"The research asks how financial protection compares with the cost of changed medical spending.","pages":"2–3","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Risk and incentives","revises":["insurance-question"]},{"label":"","title":"One employer offered four deductible plans.","content":"The $350 plan charges 20% after the deductible and caps patient payments at $2,100. At $1,000 of total medical spending, the patient pays $480.","pages":"19–20; payment arithmetic constructed","role":"setup","shortTitle":"An actual contract","panels":[{"kind":"insurance-bill","title":"At $1,000 of total medical spending, the patient pays $480.","label":"ONE EMPLOYER · FOUR OFFERED PLANS","lines":[],"note":"Premium excluded","data":{"totalBill":1000,"deductible":350,"postDeductibleBill":650,"postDeductiblePatientPayment":130,"patientPayment":480,"insurerPayment":520},"revises":["insurance-arithmetic","insurance-contracts","insurance-data"]}]},{"label":"","title":"The contract gives medical care three marginal prices.","content":"Patient payments rise at $1, then 20¢, then $0 per additional dollar of care. These payments determine the money left for other goods.","pages":"4, 7–9, 13–16","role":"setup","shortTitle":"From contract to budget","panels":[{"kind":"insurance-schedule","title":"The same contract has three marginal prices.","label":"THE CONTRACT’S PAYMENT SCHEDULE","lines":[],"note":"The $2,100 payment cap is reached at $9,100 of total medical spending.","data":{"xLabel":"Total medical spending ($), patient + insurer","yLabel":"Patient payment ($), excluding premium","vertices":[[0,0],[350,350],[9100,2100],[12000,2100]],"markedPoint":[1000,480],"insetXDomain":[0,1000],"insetYDomain":[0,500],"xDomain":[0,12000],"yDomain":[0,2400]},"revises":["insurance-arithmetic","insurance-contracts"]},{"kind":"equation","title":"Medical payments leave less money for other goods.","label":"FROM CONTRACT TO MODEL","formula":"A+P(Q)=Y-m","lines":["\\(Q\\): total medical spending · \\(P(Q)\\): patient payment","\\(A\\): other goods · \\(Y\\): income · \\(m\\): premium","The contract determines how much money remains for other goods."],"note":"Plan chosen before the health shock; care chosen afterward.","revises":["insurance-budget","insurance-timing"]}]},{"label":"","title":"The model separates risk protection from spending incentives.","content":"Plan choice occurs before the health shock; medical spending is chosen after it. The model computes both sides of the welfare comparison.","pages":"7, 10–12","role":"setup","shortTitle":"The welfare comparison","revises":["insurance-timing"]},{"label":"","title":"Data and model determine the estimated comparison.","content":"Estimation links contract choices and spending to model parameters. The predictions reproduce some spending patterns and miss others.","pages":"15, 17–18, 21–31","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Estimation and fit","revises":["insurance-estimation","insurance-fit"]},{"label":"","title":"The same population can face a different contract.","content":"Counterfactuals compare specified existing and hypothetical plans, with outcomes depending on the estimated model.","pages":"32–38","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Counterfactuals","revises":["insurance-counterfactuals"]},{"label":"","title":"In this sample, moral-hazard costs exceed risk-protection gains.","content":"For the existing plans, the average model-implied spending distortion outweighs the gain from financial protection. The sample is already insured and does not include large expenditure shocks.","pages":"19, 39–42; support 43–46","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Findings and scope","takeaway":"The welfare comparison varies substantially across individuals.","revises":["insurance-finding"]}],"change":"Introduce the observed contract and its payment arithmetic before the general budget and choice model.","overviewTitle":"What does health insurance buy?"},"topic":"Health insurance"}}]
